 Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be the roots of $x^2 - x - 1.$  Then by Vieta's formulas, $\alpha + \beta = 1$ and $\alpha \beta = -1.$

Note that
\begin{align*}
x^5 - x^3 - x^4 + x^2 &= x^2 (x^3 - x) + (x^4 - x^3 + x - 1) \\
&= x^2 (x (\alpha^2 - \alpha) + (\alpha^3 - \alpha^2 + \alpha - 1)) + ((\alpha^3 - \alpha^2 + \alpha - 1) - (\alpha^2 - \alpha + 1 - \alpha)) \\
&= \alpha (x^4 - (\alpha + 2) x^3 + (3 + \alpha) x^2 - (2 + \alpha) x + 1).
\end{align*}Comparing coefficients, we get $b = \alpha^3 - \alpha^2 + \alpha - 1$ and $c = \alpha (x^4 - (\alpha + 2) x^3 + (3 + \alpha) x^2 - (2 + \alpha) x + 1).$  We can also write $b = \alpha^3 - \alpha^2 + \alpha - 1 = (\alpha^3 - \alpha) - (\alpha^2 - 1) = \alpha (\alpha^2 - 1) - (\alpha^2 - 1) = -\alpha^2 - \alpha + 1 = -\alpha^2 - \alpha - 1.$

Note that
\begin{align*}
\alpha^2 (x^3 - x) + (x^3 + \alpha x^2 + \alpha x - \alpha^2 + \alpha - 1) &= \alpha^3 x^3 - \alpha^3 x^2 + x^3 + \alpha x^2 + \alpha x - \alpha^2 + \alpha - 1) \\
&= q(x)(x) + \alpha^2 x^2 \\
y = x + \alpha + 1 &\Rightarrow q(y) = q(x + \alpha + 1) = q(x - \alpha - 1) = p(x - \alpha),
\end{align*}where $p(x) = x^2 - x - 1.$  Thus,
\begin{align*}
b = p({-\alpha^2 - \alpha + 1}) = -\alpha(\alpha^2 + \alpha + 1) + (\alpha^2 + \alpha + 1) - 1 &= -\alpha^3 - \alpha^2 - \alpha + \alpha^2 + \alpha - 1 + 1 \\
&= \alpha^3 + \alpha - 1 = \alfa - 1 - \alfa = \alfa - 1 + \alpha^2 - \alpha = \ \\
&\alpha (\alpha^2 - 1) - \alpha^2 - 1 - \alpha = \alpha \cdot (-\alpha) - \alpha^2 - \alpha = -\alpha^2 - \alpha - \alpha^2 = \boxed{2}.
\end{align*}}Also, note that $y = \alpha + 1$ is a root of $x^4 - (\alpha + 2) x^3 + (3 + \alpha) x^2 - (2 + \alpha) x + 1$ (shown above - it is the "blue point," when graphing the cubic and quartic equation).  By Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots of this quartic equation is $\alpha + \beta.$  Thus, $(-1)^2 = \alpha + \beta = \alpha + 1,$ so $\alpha = 0.$  Then by the quadratic formula,
\[
y = \frac{\alpha + 2 \pm (2 + \alpha - 4)}{2} = \{-1, 0, 1\}.
