Let $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}.$

Then
\[\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \cdot 3 - (-6) \cdot 1 \\ (-6) \cdot 1 - 5 \cdot 3 \\ 5 \cdot 1 - 2 \cdot 1 \end{pmatrix} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ -21 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}}.\](From C) if we take $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix},$ then
\[\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \det \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{1} \\ 5 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ -6 & 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \det \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\ -6 & 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{1} \times \mathbf{b}) = \mathbf{b} \cdot (\mathbf{1} \times \mathbf{a}).\]We also note that if we take $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ -21 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix},$ then $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{1} = \mathbf{1} \times \mathbf{a} = \mathbf{0},$ so in particular, $$\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{1} \times \mathbf{b}) = \mathbf{b} \cdot (\mathbf{1} \times \mathbf{a}) = 0,$$which shows that $\mathbf{a}$ must lie in the plane formed by $\mathbf1$ and $\mathbf{b}.$  In other words, $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}$ have the same direction, so that $\mathbf{a} \cross \mathbf{b} = \mathbf{a}.$

